db/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497322 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497322 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497322 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497322 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "feee918a-d250-5e87-b14c-0c111d8be8f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-0.25x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54e98471-5688-5240-867b-3e9eae0b3046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497359 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3xy=0 \\\\ -y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 4x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)(1-0.25(1))-3(1)y & =0 \\\\ 3-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af33fe07-4f86-5eae-9df8-9d1a0ae2b710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497388 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-2xy)=2-4x-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+3xy)=-2+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-2y & -2x \\\\ 3y & -2+3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0)-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 3(0) & -2+3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f33a0982-c501-5349-ad00-235127306e08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497413 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=5x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1-2x)-xy)=5-20x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+3xy)=-2+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5-20x-y & -x \\\\ 3y & -2+3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}5-20(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 3(0) & -2+3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497435 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497435 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51e6cd51-40b0-57dc-ae60-04efd51a77f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497435 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497437 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5932a145-7c28-5526-882d-17efb83e0fb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497437 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497459 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497459 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c6eefcd-5b2a-58f2-8fa4-3bbf0349123c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497459 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186370416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497473 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497473 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bdfcda17-c79f-53ce-af4f-1ad4c7ab8581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497473 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497359 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
